---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import TriggerGenerator from '../../components/TriggerGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="SQL Trigger Template Generator" description="Generate a CREATE TRIGGER statement template from table, timing, event, and body.">
  <TriggerGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why triggers are powerful but easy to overuse</h2>
  <p>Triggers run invisibly whenever their condition is met, which makes debugging harder — a developer looking at application code has no way to know a database-level trigger is also firing and modifying data, unless they specifically know to check for it.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose the table, timing (BEFORE/AFTER), and event (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE).</li>
    <li>Write the trigger body logic.</li>
    <li>Adapt the syntax for your specific database, since trigger syntax varies notably between engines.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A BEFORE UPDATE trigger on a users table might automatically set an updated_at timestamp column whenever a row is modified, without requiring application code to remember to set it explicitly.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using triggers for logic that belongs in application code</strong>, making behavior harder to trace, test, and version control effectively.</li>
    <li><strong>Not documenting trigger existence</strong> clearly, causing confusion when a developer can't figure out why data is changing unexpectedly.</li>
    <li><strong>Creating recursive trigger chains</strong> (one trigger's action fires another trigger) without realizing it, causing unexpected cascading effects.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Automatically maintaining an updated_at timestamp column</li>
    <li>Enforcing complex data integrity rules that can't be expressed as simple constraints</li>
    <li>Logging changes to an audit table automatically</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is trigger syntax the same across databases?</strong> No — MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server have meaningfully different trigger syntax (PostgreSQL, for instance, requires a separate function definition), so adapt the generated template for your specific database.</p>
</ToolLayout>
